# Named `Dockerfile.deploy.dockerignore` because that is the name Docker reads:
# BuildKit looks for `<dockerfile>.dockerignore` beside the Dockerfile and falls
# back to `.dockerignore`. This file was called `.dockerignore.deploy`, which
# matches neither, so every rule below was inert and `frontend/.dockerignore`
# applied instead — and that one excludes `.next`, which is the entire payload
# Dockerfile.deploy copies. Building this image from a repository checkout
# therefore failed on `.next/static: not found`. (Deploys from the tarball were
# unaffected: the bundle ships no ignore file at all, so Docker applied none.)
#
# Build context for Dockerfile.deploy, which copies a build made on the
# developer's machine rather than building inside the image — the server cannot
# reach the npm registry reliably.
#
# The difference from .dockerignore is .next. That file excludes it, correctly:
# the normal Dockerfile builds from source, so a stale .next would only slow the
# context transfer. Here .next IS the payload, and excluding it makes the COPY
# lines fail with ".next/static: not found".
.env*
.git
.gitignore

# The project's own dependencies are not needed — but the standalone build
# carries its own minimal node_modules and that IS needed at runtime. Negated
# explicitly rather than relying on whether a bare pattern matches nested
# paths: getting that wrong builds an image that only fails once it runs.
node_modules
!.next/standalone/node_modules

.next/cache
out
build
*.log
.turbo
coverage
